WI-kozos. Raised by the owner: "for some arbitrary code occurring anywhere
within the hypergumbo repo, be it in a .py file or in a .txt or .md file, do
we have a way to guarantee that that code does or does not run?" The answer is
no, and the sharper finding was that the artifact never SAID which execution
context it was talking about.

WHAT IT ACTUALLY COVERS. Every claim takes a python cmd_* handler as its taint
source, so all 18 describe one thing: what the installed CLI does when a user
runs it. The file opened with "hypergumbo's safety claims are checked by
running hypergumbo against its own source", which a reader can fairly take as
a claim about the REPOSITORY. It is not one. The narrower statement is both
true and load-bearing for everything else in the file, so it is now stated
first.

A table names the four uncovered contexts rather than leaving them to be
inferred from an absence: pytest inside the repo, git/agent hooks, CI, and
hypergumbo ANALYSING a repo (the rust-analyzer backend executes the target's
build.rs).

THREE THINGS SAID OUT LOUD BECAUSE IMPLYING THEM WOULD BE THE OVERCLAIM:

  * conftest.py executes at COLLECTION -- `pytest --collect-only` runs it with
    no test selected, before any selection logic could exclude it. The root
    conftest legitimately subprocesses and re-execs the interpreter (ADR-0010's
    self-healing wrapper), so the capability is live BY DESIGN, not
    hypothetical.

  * WHICH THREAT MODEL THIS SERVES. The audit discards flows sourced in test
    fixtures, on the reasoning that a fixture reaching a real primitive is a
    fixture doing its job. Correct for "is the shipped tool safe to run";
    exactly backwards for "is this repository safe to run tests in". The
    artifact never stated which question it answered, so a reader could take a
    verdict as evidence for the other one.

  * THE .md/.txt FINDING IS CONFIGURATION, NOT STRUCTURE. Those files are not
    collected as tests -- verified, along with no eval()/exec() on file content
    anywhere in packages/*/src and CSafeLoader throughout. But one line in
    pyproject.toml revokes the collection rule, so it is recorded as a
    configuration fact. Filing it as a structural guarantee is precisely the
    move this section exists to prevent, and it is the honest answer to the
    question that prompted the item.

PLACEMENT IS DELIBERATE: the section renders BEFORE the measured-verdict
table. A reader needs to know what was examined before being told whether it
passed -- particularly here, where the answer is 18 of 18 inconclusive.

Generated, not hand-written: it lives in scripts/generate-security-md, so it
cannot drift from the file the CI freshness check regenerates. Six tests pin
the content that matters (the covered context named, every uncovered context
present, collection-time execution, the threat-model sentence, and
configuration-not-structure) rather than pinning prose byte-for-byte, so
rewording stays cheap and dropping a disclosure does not.

STILL OPEN, and flagged on the item as the owner's call rather than resolved
here: whether a scope statement is sufficient, or whether any of the uncovered
contexts should get claims of its own. That is a threat-model decision, not a
bug fix, and it is part of the INV-dabuf package.

